{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"71619a630dda6b78","name":"card_event_romanian_bridgehead","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Poland/events/card_event_romanian_bridgehead","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"ROMANIAN BRIDGEHEAD","text":"Give target unit +2+2 and Blitz. Destroy it in 2 turns.","flavorText":"The Romanian Bridgehead was a strategic fallback plan for the Polish Army during WWII, designed as a last resort in case of a full-scale German and Soviet invasion. The plan involved retreating Polish forces to southeastern Poland, near the border with Romania, in a region that is now part of Ukraine. The area’s rugged terrain, rivers, and natural defenses were expected to slow enemy advances, allowing Poland to regroup and receive potential aid from Britain and France through Romanian ports. However, the Soviet invasion on September 17th, 1939, alongside the ongoing German offensive, made the plan impossible to execute effectively. As a result, many Polish government officials, military leaders, and troops crossed into Romania, where they were interned, but they later escaped to continue fighting with the Allies. Despite its failure, the Romanian Bridgehead remains a key part of Poland’s WWII defense strategy.","faction":"Poland","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_romanian_bridgehead","title":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_romanian_bridgehead_title","SourceString":"ROMANIAN BRIDGEHEAD","LocalizedString":"ROMANIAN BRIDGEHEAD"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Poland","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_romanian_bridgehead_text","SourceString":"Give target unit +2+2 and Blitz. Destroy it in 2 turns.","LocalizedString":"Give target unit +2+2 and Blitz. Destroy it in 2 turns."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_romanian_bridgehead_history","SourceString":"The Romanian Bridgehead was a strategic fallback plan for the Polish Army during WWII, designed as a last resort in case of a full-scale German and Soviet invasion. The plan involved retreating Polish forces to southeastern Poland, near the border with Romania, in a region that is now part of Ukraine. The area’s rugged terrain, rivers, and natural defenses were expected to slow enemy advances, allowing Poland to regroup and receive potential aid from Britain and France through Romanian ports. However, the Soviet invasion on September 17th, 1939, alongside the ongoing German offensive, made the plan impossible to execute effectively. As a result, many Polish government officials, military leaders, and troops crossed into Romania, where they were interned, but they later escaped to continue fighting with the Allies. Despite its failure, the Romanian Bridgehead remains a key part of Poland’s WWII defense strategy.","LocalizedString":"The Romanian Bridgehead was a strategic fallback plan for the Polish Army during WWII, designed as a last resort in case of a full-scale German and Soviet invasion. The plan involved retreating Polish forces to southeastern Poland, near the border with Romania, in a region that is now part of Ukraine. The area’s rugged terrain, rivers, and natural defenses were expected to slow enemy advances, allowing Poland to regroup and receive potential aid from Britain and France through Romanian ports. However, the Soviet invasion on September 17th, 1939, alongside the ongoing German offensive, made the plan impossible to execute effectively. As a result, many Polish government officials, military leaders, and troops crossed into Romania, where they were interned, but they later escaped to continue fighting with the Allies. Despite its failure, the Romanian Bridgehead remains a key part of Poland’s WWII defense strategy."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BloodAndIron","usedTriggers":["ERegistered